{"name":"Love Goddess","hex":"#cd0d0d","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"love","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A deep caring for the existence of another.","Strong affection."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To have a strong affection for (someone or something).","To need, thrive on."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/love"},{"term":"goddess","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A female deity.","An incredibly beautiful, gorgeous, sexy, attractive woman honored and adored for her beauty or of superior charm and intelligence."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/goddess"}],"entries":[{"title":"List of love and lust deities","blocks":[{"text":"A love deity or lust deity is a deity in mythology associated with romance, sex, love, lust, or sexuality. Love deities are common in mythology and are found in many polytheistic religions. Female sex goddesses are often associated with beauty and other traditionally feminine attributes."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/90/Kama_Rati_sculpture_at_the_group_of_monuments_at_Belur%2C_Hassan%2C_Karnataka%2C_India_%282009%29.jpg/330px-Kama_Rati_sculpture_at_the_group_of_monuments_at_Belur%2C_Hassan%2C_Karnataka%2C_India_%282009%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List_of_love_and_lust_deities","wikidata":"Q11917906"},{"title":"Love","blocks":[{"text":"Love encompasses a range of strong and positive emotional and mental states, from the most sublime virtue or good habit, the deepest interpersonal affection, to the simplest pleasure. An example of this range of meanings is that the love of a mother differs from the love of a spouse, which differs from the love for food. Most commonly, love refers to a feeling of strong attraction and emotional attachment."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Love","wikidata":"Q316"},{"title":"Goddess","blocks":[{"text":"A goddess is a female deity. In some faiths, a sacred female figure holds a central place in religious prayer and worship. For example, Shaktism, holds that the ultimate deity, the source of all reality, is Supreme Goddess (Mahamaya) and in some forms of Tantric Shaivism, the pair of Shiva and Shakti are the ultimate principle. Meanwhile, in Vajrayana Buddhism, ultimate reality is often seen as being composed of two principles depicted as two deities in union symbolising the non-duality of the two principles of perfect wisdom (female) and skillful compassion (male). A single figure in a monotheistic faith that is female may be identified simply as god because of no need to differentiate by gender or with a diminutive. An experiment to determine the effect of psychedelics on subjects composed of leaders from diverse religious groups revealed a general experience that the divine which the subjects encountered was feminine."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/39/Queen_Nefertari_being_led_by_Isis_MET_DP167142.jpg/330px-Queen_Nefertari_being_led_by_Isis_MET_DP167142.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Goddess","wikidata":"Q205985"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/79/L%27Aurore_MR_3243.jpg/500px-L%27Aurore_MR_3243.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/79/L%27Aurore_MR_3243.jpg/500px-L%27Aurore_MR_3243.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Statue of Eos","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AL'Aurore_MR_3243.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0d/Eros_Farnese_N%C3%A1poles_01.jpg/500px-Eros_Farnese_N%C3%A1poles_01.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thum